WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.640
Move a mussel and a shore crab from seawater into water diluted to half strength.

2
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Whose blood follows the water?

3
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.460
The first film built a dial: push-back strength against leak strength.

4
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.720
Now we read that dial off real animals.

5
00:00:20.833 --> 00:00:27.593
Draw a plot. Across the bottom goes the water's osmolality, the concentration of

6
00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:28.593
dissolved particles.

7
00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:32.133
Up the side goes the blood's osmolality.

8
00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:36.773
Orange marks the outside, teal the inside, as before.

9
00:00:37.917 --> 00:00:39.557
Draw the diagonal first.

10
00:00:41.077 --> 00:00:42.397
Along it, blood equals water.

11
00:00:43.637 --> 00:00:45.117
That is a perfect conformer.

12
00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:47.677
Then draw a flat line.

13
00:00:48.977 --> 00:00:51.557
Along it, the blood stays put as the water changes.

14
00:00:52.857 --> 00:00:56.117
That is a perfect regulator, over the range it survives.

15
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.370
The slope of an animal's line is the share of the outside change that gets inside.

16
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:06.650
In the simple model, steep means leak is much stronger.

17
00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:09.870
Flat means push-back is much stronger.

18
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:14.670
A line halfway between means the two are about equally strong.

19
00:01:15.783 --> 00:01:18.363
Now the blue mussel, Mytilus edulis.

20
00:01:18.943 --> 00:01:24.723
Dilute its water, and over the salinities it tolerates, its blood comes close to the

21
00:01:24.723 --> 00:01:28.623
water around it. Its points fall along the diagonal.

22
00:01:29.943 --> 00:01:32.603
For blood osmolality, the mussel conforms.

23
00:01:33.703 --> 00:01:35.663
That label covers this one variable.

24
00:01:36.733 --> 00:01:39.093
Now the shore crab, Carcinus maenas.

25
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:44.813
In full-strength seawater, its blood sits close to the water, near the diagonal.

26
00:01:46.193 --> 00:01:48.453
So far, the crab looks just like the mussel.

27
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:51.683
Now dilute the water to half strength.

28
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:57.023
Will the crab's blood sit on the diagonal, on the flat line, or in between?

29
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.410
In between. The crab holds its blood well above the water.

30
00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:11.870
Its line bends flatter in dilute water.

31
00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.110
So the crab conforms at one end of its range and regulates at the other.

32
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:22.723
So regulator and conformer describe one variable over one range.

33
00:02:23.923 --> 00:02:25.743
They never describe a whole animal.

34
00:02:27.163 --> 00:02:30.683
Name one variable and a stated range before you choose a label.

35
00:02:31.817 --> 00:02:33.937
Now a second plot, for temperature.

36
00:02:35.317 --> 00:02:36.977
Outside temperature goes across.

37
00:02:38.437 --> 00:02:39.737
Body temperature goes up.

38
00:02:40.877 --> 00:02:45.497
Over the air temperatures a person can bear, an adult human regulates core

39
00:02:45.497 --> 00:02:48.837
temperature. That gives a nearly flat line.

40
00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:53.227
A typical fish in a lake gives a line near the diagonal.

41
00:02:54.587 --> 00:02:57.287
Its body temperature largely follows the water.

42
00:02:58.400 --> 00:02:59.900
Two plots, one dial.

43
00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:05.140
Each line's slope is the share of an outside change that gets inside.

44
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.900
It holds for that variable, over that range.

45
00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.740
In the simple model, that slope reads the dial.
